{"classified_as":[{"_label":"Collection Item","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404024","type":"Type"}],"id":"https://data.okeeffemuseum.org/touring-exhibition/884","identified_by":[{"classified_as":[{"_label":"preferred term","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670"}],"content":"Joan Didion: What She Means","type":"Name"}],"produced_by":{"carried_out_by":[{"_label":"Armand Hammer Museum of Art","id":"actor/2073","type":"Actor"}],"timespan":{"type":"TimeSpan"},"type":"Production"},"referred_to_by":[{"classified_as":[{"_label":"description statement","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416"}],"content":"Joan Didion: What She Means considers the visual corollary to Joan Didion’s life and work as a writer.  Didion’s essay on Georgia O’Keeffe in The White Album (1979) didn’t shy away from the personal, with her daughter’s magnetic pull towards Georgia O’Keeffe’s paintings opening up a window into the artist’s work.  The loan request of the Todd Webb photograph of O’Keeffe begins to fill out this era in Didion’s life.","type":"LinguisticObject"}],"representation":[],"type":"Activity"}